Google Vids, the company's video tool, now generates clips with its Omni 1.2 Flash model, and Google says anyone with a Google account can try it at no cost. For a small business that could mean a free way to make a short product teaser or social clip. The catch is that Google's own pages disagree about how much "free" is. This guide sets out what each page says, which to trust, and how to make a first clip.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited Google pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test Google Vids, and has no affiliate relationship with Google at the time of writing. Everything here comes from Google's own pages, read on 15 November 2026. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Google announced

The announcement is dated 5 November 2026. It says Vids now runs on Omni 1.2 Flash and that anyone with a Google account or a Google Workspace subscription can generate up to three 15-second videos a month in Vids at no cost. The post does not mention paid tiers or other plan differences. It adds that reference images keep characters and scenes consistent across a clip, and that speech narration sounds more natural.

## The free allowance: three Google pages, three answers

Here is what each official page says. None of the help pages shows a date, so the order below is not a timeline.

- **Announcement (5 November 2026):** up to three 15-second videos a month, counted in videos. Three clips of 15 seconds is 45 seconds in total, by simple arithmetic; the post gives no seconds figure itself.
- **Help page on Gemini availability in Vids:** a table of monthly limits by account type. A personal Google account is listed at 60 seconds of AI video clips and six AI avatar generations. Higher figures are listed for paid plans, for example 200 seconds for Business Starter and 500 seconds for Google AI Pro. Personal and AI Plus accounts are also described as getting 50 credits a month, and the page says personal accounts might have some restrictions on AI video editing.
- **Help page on generating video clips:** says most users can generate up to 50 videos a month, with a duration of 3 to 15 seconds for text-to-video clips (shorter for animated images and edits), and that the limit resets at 12 AM Pacific time on the first of the month. It says the feature needs an eligible Google Workspace subscription and ties it to the Google Workspace Experiments trusted tester program. That may explain why its 50-video figure differs from the others, but that is our inference, not something Google states.
- **Help page on avatars:** says the number of avatar generations varies by plan, and that some plans share one limit between avatars and AI video clips.

![Three-column comparison of what the Google Vids announcement and two Google help pages say about the monthly AI video allowance](/images/site1/google-vids-free-ai-video-limits-and-how-to-start-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited Google pages. The pages are undated and do not agree, so treat every figure as subject to change.*

**Which should you trust?** Whatever Vids itself shows for your account, backed by the help page for your account type, because allowances vary by plan and Google can change them. Treat the announcement figure as a headline. We could not tell from the pages whether the differences come from timing, account type or something else. If you are on Workspace, your admin can turn these features off, and Frontline plans do not include them, according to the availability page.

## How to start

1. Open **vids.new** in a desktop browser. The announcement links it as the place to try Vids; it does not say whether mobile works.
2. Sign in with your Google account and check that the AI clip option is available to you.
3. Choose the Omni option in the clip settings. The help page says that is where duration, aspect ratio and resolution are set. It lists 16:9 or 9:16, 720p or 1080p and 24 frames per second.
4. Note how many clips you make, since the allowance depends on your account and no source says Vids shows a remaining-allowance counter.

## Reference images and narration

Reference images are the most useful feature for a business. Upload a product photo or a character sketch and Google says the characters and scenes stay consistent from first frame to last. Google's developer post describes reference inputs as character, product or style images, but it does not state how many you can add.

Narration is described as more natural in Vids, with music mentioned in the developer post. The pages give no list of voices or settings, so check the options in the tool itself.

## What the same model costs developers

If you build your own tools, Google's developer post lists the model at $0.07 per second of 720p video output, which it says is 30% lower than Omni 1.1. At that rate a 15-second clip would cost about $1.05, our own arithmetic. We found no per-second price for other resolutions. We could not confirm the figure on the Gemini API pricing page, whose excerpt we read did not include the Omni 1.2 Flash section. This price applies to the developer API, not to Vids.

## Your first 15-second clip

1. **Write a short script.** Aim for one idea, such as one product and one benefit. Fifteen seconds is about two or three short sentences of narration.
2. **Pick one reference image.** Use a clear, well-lit photo of the product or a simple character you own the rights to. Avoid images of real people unless they have agreed.
3. **Set the clip.** Choose 15 seconds, the aspect ratio for where it will run, and the resolution.
4. **Generate, then review closely.** Check the product's shape, colours, any text on screen and what the narration claims. AI video can invent details, so a clip that shows a feature your product lacks is a problem for you, not Google.
5. **Label it.** If you publish it, say it is AI-generated, for example in the caption, and first read the commercial-use section below.

## Labelling AI video in ads

Be honest with your audience. A clip that looks like real footage of your shop or customers, but is generated, can mislead. Say plainly when a video is AI-generated, and never use generated footage to show results or testimonials that did not happen. The developer post says outputs carry an imperceptible SynthID watermark, but the Vids help pages we read do not mention it, and a hidden watermark is not a label your viewers can see. Platforms and countries have their own advertising rules; check yours.

## Commercial use: check before you advertise

We did not find a clear statement on commercial use. The clip-generation help page says generated images and videos are "for use only within Vids". Read literally, that could limit using the clips outside Vids, which would matter for ads and social posts, and Google does not clarify on that page. It also says use is subject to Google's Generative AI Prohibited Use Policy. Before putting a clip in a paid ad or selling it, read the current terms for your account type, and ask Google or a lawyer if the answer is unclear.

## Unverified

- The 60-second help figure could not be traced to a dated version.
- Whether the free allowance is three videos, 45 seconds, 60 seconds or 50 videos for your account.
- The Omni 1.2 Flash price on the live API pricing page.
- Anything about commercial use beyond the sentence quoted above.
